 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions reach connected browsers over WebSocket connections this project runs. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it meets the freshness target with the simplest stateless operation, but at the cost of repeated reads and a few seconds of lag. A hosted realtime service was rejected: the quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection operations, but at the cost of recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets were chosen accepting the work of owning connection capacity and reconnect problems. Full trade-off record: [`docs/decisions/realtime-change-delivery.md`](decisions/realtime-change-delivery.md).
